Encephalocele
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Midline cranial protrusion, Skull defect on imaging, Hydrocephalus, Delayed development

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-019
Reason for Visit: Acute skin breakdown and suspected cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak over a cranial defect.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 2-month-old infant with a known occipital encephalocele presenting with new-onset clear fluid leaking from the midline cranial mass, accompanied by increased irritability. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the extremely high risk of ascending bacterial meningitis and acute hydrocephalus decompensation, requiring immediate clinical evaluation of the defect's integrity.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin over the bump on the back of his head looks rubbed raw, and there is clear fluid dripping from it.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 4-hour history of clear, watery fluid leaking from the visible midline cranial mass located in the occipital region. The infant has a known occipital encephalocele and secondary microcephaly, pending definitive neurosurgical repair. Today, the parents noticed a small area of skin breakdown over the sac-like protrusion, likely caused by friction against the crib mattress. Concurrently, the infant has become increasingly irritable, with episodes of high-pitched crying, but no frank seizures or abnormal neurological posturing have been observed yet. The infant has a history of delayed motor development.
Functional Status: Fully dependent infant; requires specialized positioning to avoid pressure on the cranial defect.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of life-threatening central nervous system infection (meningitis/ventriculitis).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 80/45 mmHg | HR: 160 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 38 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 4.5 Kg
General: Infant is irritable, crying inconsolably when lying supine, but calms slightly when held prone.
Physical Exam: Head exam reveals a large, visible midline cranial mass in the occipital region, covered by a thin membrane. There is a 1cm area of excoriation at the apex of the protrusion. Active leakage of clear, watery fluid is observed from this excoriation, highly suspicious for cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). The anterior fontanelle is tense but not overtly bulging. Neurological exam reveals hyperreflexia in the lower limbs and generalized irritability. Pupils are equal and reactive. No nuchal rigidity is appreciable, though assessment is limited by the mass.
Environmental Observations: Standard flat crib mattress in use without a specialized pressure-relieving donut pillow.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Encephalocele (complicated by acute CSF leak and suspected impending meningitis) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Ruptured Epidermal Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to the known anatomical defect, the clear, watery nature of the fluid (rather than sebaceous material), and the tense fontanelle suggesting communicating fluid dynamics.

Superficial Skin Excoriation (Without CSF leak) - Ruled out/unlikely given the continuous, pulsatile flow of the clear fluid corresponding with the infant's crying, confirming a breach into the subarachnoid space.

Established Bacterial Meningitis - Not entirely ruled out, but lacking the classic signs of high fever or profound lethargy currently; however, the risk is imminent.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Neurosurgical emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The infant presented with an actively leaking occipital encephalocele and increasing irritability. The objective finding of clear fluid leaking from an excoriated cranial defect in the context of a tense fontanelle strongly confirms a CSF leak. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because an open pathway to the brain and meninges guarantees rapid onset of ascending bacterial meningitis and catastrophic changes in intracranial pressure (hydrocephalus). Immediate emergency department transfer is mandatory for neurosurgical evaluation, IV prophylactic antibiotics, and potential emergency closure.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied a sterile, non-adherent saline-soaked dressing lightly over the leaking defect to minimize gross contamination without applying pressure that could force fluid back into the cranial vault. Kept the infant strictly in a lateral decubitus position. Activated 000 for immediate emergency ambulance transfer to the Paediatric Neurosurgery center.
Positive Behavioral Change: N/A - Acute neurosurgical emergency requiring immediate transfer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedic crew and receiving neurosurgical registrar.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported to the Emergency Department via ambulance under my direct supervision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A CSF leak is a life-threatening complication that requires immediate hospital-based management.
Clinical Course: The infant will require admission, likely for intravenous antibiotics and potentially an expedited neurosurgical repair of the defect to close the leak.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not apply any pressure to the mass. Do not attempt to feed the infant, as they may require emergency surgery under general anesthesia.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The thin skin over the brain sac has broken open, and the fluid that cushions the brain is leaking out. This is highly dangerous because bacteria can travel up that fluid and infect the brain directly. He must go to the hospital immediately for specialized antibiotics and surgery.
Actionable Steps: Keep the baby on his side to avoid putting any pressure on the back of the head. Pack a bag for a hospital stay. Follow all instructions from the paramedics.

